  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nA 595 dm high has expanded over Southern California to New Mexico \ntoday. Models indicate this pressure system not moving very much \nthrough the work week, leading to minor day to day changes in the \noverall weather pattern. Temperatures will go down a touch through \nthe middle of the week, but remain close to average for the coastal \nareas and adjacent valleys, with highs 5-10 degrees above normal for \ninland regions. Coastal low clouds will move into areas within 10 \nmiles of the coast each night and morning as the marine layer depth \nremains relatively unchanged this week.\n\nSubtle movements in the high's position, will dictate a change in \nthe monsoonal moisture pattern. By Wednesday into the weekend, \nmodels indicate a slight shift in the high closer to the Four \nCorners region with better easterly flow aloft. This is looking more \nlikely to occur, which will increase humidity overall for everyone; \nwe will also see increasing yet subtle chances for showers and weak \nthunderstorms over the peninsular (Julian to Big Bear) mountain \nspine. Chances remain minimal to none by Thursday and Friday. Model \nagreement begins to diverge some on where the high exactly ends up \nby the weekend into early next week, somewhere between southern \nNevada and the Four Corners. Ensemble members focus the best chance \nfor rainfall by this weekend into early next week, but confidence on \nany rainfall occurring is low at this time. The high may strengthen \nagain by later this week as well, which would increase temperatures \nslightly yet again. Our message remains the same that elevated heat \nrisk continues through the week!",
